{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Note that the terms alternate in sign and have **even** factorials in the denominator. This suggests that we can use the Maclaurin series for $\\cos x$ as a guide.   Recall that \n\n$\\displaystyle\\cos x=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^n\\frac{x^{2n}}{(2n)!}\\,$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"Hence\n\n$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^{n}\\frac{x^{6n}}{(2n)!}=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^n\\frac{(x^3)^{2n}}{(2n)!}=\\cos \\big(x^3\\big)$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"Now substitute $~x = \\displaystyle\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\,$.\n\n$\\displaystyle\\cos \\big(x^3\\big)= \\cos\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^3\\right)=\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{2}=0$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"$f$ is the Maclaurin series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^{n}\\frac{x^{6n}}{(2n)!}$.\n\n$f\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}\\right)=$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"maxError":null,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":0}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"input answer","size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
